sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 native Swamp schedules, one Swamp workflow per Schedule (Nick 2026-11-02), nothing installed on Nodes; R1-R3.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: schedule:create
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh
  CLI->>GW: schedule:create report<br/>--instance=42 --calendar=@daily
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit schedule:create report --instance=42 --calendar='0 3 * * *' --command='php artisan report:send' --timeout=600`
  %%> - syntax: `<name> (--node=ID | --instance=ID | --project=ID --for=production) --calendar=CALENDAR --command=COMMAND [--timeout=SECONDS] [--no-start] [--idempotency-key=KEY]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the name as the argument, unique for its Node or Instance
  %%> - exactly one owner: a Node, an Instance, or a Project definition
  %%> - the calendar, the command line and the timeout (default 3,600 seconds)
  %%> Calendar:
  %%> - a cron expression, the format Swamp schedules use, for example `0 3 * * *`, a 6-field form with seconds such as `20 * * * * *`, or a shorthand such as `@hourly` or `@daily`
  %%> - it fires in serve's time zone (UTC); a Swamp trigger carries no time zone
  %%> - existing systemd calendars are converted once during the migration
  %%> - optionally `--no-start`, to create the Schedule disabled
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request
  Note over GW: Validate, record Schedule<br/>Schedule 7f3c… · Instance 42
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs Instance 42
  %%> - Instance 42 is active; a production Instance has a selected release
  %%> - the name, calendar, command and timeout are valid
  %%> - Instance 42 has no Schedule with this name and another specification (`schedule.retry_conflict`)
  %%> Derives from the Placement:
  %%> - the user, the working directory and the shell the command runs with
  %%> Result:
  %%> - Schedule 7f3c… recorded, with its desired state `enabled`, or `disabled` with `--no-start`
  %%> - an identical create returns the existing Schedule
  %%> With `--project`:
  %%> - only a Project Schedule definition is recorded (R1); the answer comes at once
  %%> - production preparation later copies it into a disabled Instance Schedule
  %%> Exception:
  %%> - production's per-minute schedulers (`laravel-scheduler`, `powersync-health`) keep their systemd timer on the Node until the serve load and outage test passes (ADR 0244)
  %%> - all other Node and Instance Schedules are native Swamp schedules on 209
  GW->>SW: Store the command<br/>vault: schedule-command-7f3c…
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - pushes the command line to a Swamp vault key on 209 (R3)
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the command is operator input; it never goes into workflow files, run inputs or step output
  GW->>SW: Create the workflow<br/>workflow.create schedule-7f3c…
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - creates one small Swamp workflow for this Schedule on serve (Node 209), then writes its content with `workflow.edit`
  %%> - its one step calls the shared `task-command-run` as a nested workflow
  %%> - nothing is installed on Node 12; there is no Node work, so no run starts (R1)
  %%> Fixed inside:
  %%> - the full intent: Schedule 7f3c…, Node 12's address and SSH host keys, the user, the working directory, the shell and the timeout
  %%> - the command's vault key name, never the command itself
  %%> Stored:
  %%> - in serve's `workflows/` directory beside the checked-in workflows; a release load keeps it
  %%> - the Gateway's Schedule record stays the source; an identical create writes it again
  opt not created disabled
    GW->>SW: Set its trigger<br/>workflow.trigger.set
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - sets the cron trigger of `schedule-7f3c…` on serve
    %%> When:
    %%> - skipped with `--no-start`; `schedule:enable` sets it later
  end
  alt Swamp confirms
    SW-->>GW: workflow and trigger set<br/>next fire 03:00
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the workflow exists and its trigger is active, with its next fire time
    GW-->>CLI: Schedule created<br/>active · next fire 03:00
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Schedule 7f3c… becomes active, only after Swamp confirms
    %%> - with `--no-start`: active and disabled, with no trigger
    %%> - task #5140 is recorded as succeeded at once (R1)
    %%> What you get:
    %%> - the Schedule and its next fire, at once; there is nothing to follow
  else Swamp refuses or is down
    SW--xGW: no confirmation<br/>refused or unreachable
    %%> When:
    %%> - Swamp refuses the calendar, or serve on 209 does not answer
    Note over GW: Mark the Schedule failed<br/>rerun the same create
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Schedule 7f3c… stays recorded, marked failed, with its error code
    %%> - the vault key and a created workflow without a trigger may stay; they start nothing
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same command again; an identical create writes the workflow and its trigger again
    GW--xCLI: create failed<br/>schedule.calendar_invalid or 503
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - `schedule.calendar_invalid` (422) for a refused calendar
    %%> - `swamp.schedule_publish_unavailable` (503) when serve does not answer
  end
  loop each calendar fire
    Note over SW: Start a root run<br/>schedule-7f3c…
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - serve fires the trigger and starts one root run of `schedule-7f3c…` on 209
    %%> - its step runs `task-command-run` with the fixed intent, within the Schedule's timeout
    %%> Timing:
    %%> - serve runs up to 4 scheduled runs at once; a fire can start late when all are busy
    %%> - a Schedule never overlaps itself; a fire due while the last one runs waits
    SW->>N: Run the command<br/>@swamp/ssh, as the Schedule's user
    %%> How:
    %%> - checks the user and the working directory on Node 12
    %%> - reads the command with `vault.get` and sends it on stdin
    %%> Runs as:
    %%> - the Schedule's user, in its working directory, with its shell
    N-->>SW: exit code, output
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the exit code; the output stays in the Swamp step
    SW-->>GW: Run started, finished<br/>commit feed
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the root run start and end; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record the fire<br/>task #5141 · trigger: schedule
    %%> Result:
    %%> - each fire becomes a task, with trigger `schedule`, recorded when it starts
    %%> - `last_run_at` and `last_run_status` of Schedule 7f3c… are set when it ends
  end
